UPDATES = {
    # ------------------------------------------------------------ abstract
    8: 'We mapped exons and alternative splicing events between human and mouse, and compared the use ratio of 4,570 '
       'orthologous alternative splicing events between human and mouse RNA-sequencing datasets of five immune cell '
       'types and fibroblasts.',
    9: 'Of the 1,799-2,043 alternative splicing events that were testable in each immune cell type, 18.8-21.1% were '
       'differentially spliced between species, that is, differed significantly between all human and all mouse '
       'samples by a mean difference at least twice as large as the differences between datasets of the same '
       'species. In 10.1-12.5% of the alternative splicing events the use ratio was unchanged across all datasets of '
       'both species, most of them (87%) events in which one alternative splicing outcome accounted for more than 95% '
       'of the reads, including 28 events in 21 ribosomal protein genes. Of the 858 events that were differentially '
       'spliced in at least one immune cell type and testable in fibroblasts, 35% were also differentially spliced '
       'between human and mouse fibroblasts.',
    10: 'Our results show that, among testable orthologous alternative splicing events, the use ratio is often not '
        'conserved between human and mouse immune cell types, and that robust differences are frequently shared '
        'between immune cell types and fibroblasts. By integrating exon orthology with observed splicing patterns, '
        'our analysis provides a resource for evaluating for which genes the mouse model recapitulates the '
        'alternative splicing ratio of human, and for which genes a different model should be sought.',

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ introduction
    13: 'In addition to the biological differences between human and mouse immune systems, there are also '
        'experimental and ethical considerations that impinge on studies of human and mouse immune systems (3). '
        'Studies of human immunology typically focus on cells isolated from peripheral blood, bone marrow, or cord '
        'blood, with in-vivo studies largely being limited to monitoring clinical manipulations, such as vaccinations '
        'or transplantations. In contrast, there are no such limitations on in-vivo studies in mice (10). Mouse '
        'studies are conducted primarily with inbred mouse strains that are controlled for age, environmental '
        'influences, and other confounding factors, in contrast to natural human populations for which these factors '
        'cannot always be controlled. Importantly, recent studies have shown that some differences between inbred '
        'mice and humans are due to environmental conditions, specifically microbial exposure, and not to species '
        'differences (11-13). Accordingly, some of the presumed human–mouse differences in immune systems are reduced '
        'or disappear when comparing humans to more "natural" mice, such as feral or pet-store mice (11),  inbred '
        'laboratory mice co-housed with pet-store mice (11), inbred laboratory mice born to wild-type mothers (12), '
        'or inbred laboratory mice exposed to common pathogens (13). Still, the many differences between humans and '
        'inbred laboratory mice remain a significant impediment to the translation of research from inbred '
        'laboratory mice to the clinic.',
    16: 'Analysis of RNA-seq datasets can go beyond the gene level into the different transcripts that are '
        'transcribed from each gene. Alternative splicing is a major contributor to transcriptomic and proteomic '
        'diversity in mammals and is especially prevalent in the immune system (23), where rapid adaptation and '
        'cell-type specialization require extensive post-transcriptional regulation. Alternative splicing can alter '
        'protein localization, enzymatic activity, interaction domains, or transcript stability, thereby shaping '
        'immune-cell identity and function (24).  RNA-seq technology has enabled genome-wide studies of transcript '
        'diversity. Such studies are now beginning to uncover the abundance and importance of changes in alternative '
        'splicing events during immune differentiation and activation (23-25). Few specific alternative splicing '
        'events that change use ratio have indeed been well studied [e.g., Ciita (26), Ptprc/Cd45 (27)], but, genome '
        'wide, the distribution and regulation of alternative splicing events across the immune system is far from '
        'being understood.',
    18: 'Since the prevalence of species-specific alternative splicing between human and mouse remains a '
        'controversial subject (29-31), we performed a systematic comparison of the use ratio of orthologous '
        'alternative splicing events between corresponding human and mouse immune cell types. Exon-level orthology '
        '(35), annotation-free quantification of local splicing events from junction reads (44) and cross-species '
        'comparison of splicing ratio (28-31) each have precedents; our contribution is their integration into an '
        'alternative splicing event orthology map, and applying differential splicing analysis of those events to '
        'sorted immune cell populations profiled in several independent datasets per species. There are 4,570 '
        'orthologous alternative splicing events in which both alternatives are expressed in human and mouse immune '
        'cells. Out of the events that could be tested in each cell type, the use ratio differed between species, by '
        'at least twice the difference between datasets of the same species, in about a fifth of the cases, whereas '
        'an unchanged use ratio was found mostly for events in which one isoform accounts for more than 95% of the '
        'reads.',

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ methods 2.1
    25: 'A human exon and a mouse exon are defined as orthologous exons if the number of identical aligned '
        'nucleotides is at least 50% of the length of each of the two exons. This criterion is less strict than that '
        'of ExOrthist (35), which aligns exons at the protein level and also requires conservation of the flanking '
        'intron positions and of the neighboring exons; ExOrthist was not used because it is restricted to '
        'protein-coding exons, whereas alternative splicing events often involve exons that are partly or entirely '
        'untranslated (for example alternative first and last exons). In our framework, conservation of the exon '
        'context is instead required through the RNA-seq data: a splicing junction is analyzed only if it connects two '
        'orthologous exons (see 2.3) and is covered by reads in both human and mouse. The start and end coordinates of '
        'orthologous exons are defined as orthologous exon boundaries. An exon can have more than one ortholog, so a '
        'single human exon boundary may be orthologous to more than one mouse exon boundary (and vice versa). In order '
        'to select the best orthologous boundary, all boundaries of the species with multiple orthologous boundaries '
        'were ranked using the ratio-based score described below, and the candidate with the highest score was chosen. '
        'If two or more candidates were tied for the highest score, the candidate whose exons belong to canonical '
        'transcripts (rather than non-canonical transcripts) was selected, then the candidate with the higher average '
        'identity, and if the tie remained, the first candidate in genomic coordinate order, which decided the partner '
        'of 4,794 of the 390,686 human boundaries (1.2%). The same procedure was applied in reverse when a single mouse exon '
        'boundary has more than one candidate orthologous human boundaries, and a human and a mouse boundary are '
        'paired only if each is the best candidate of the other (mutual best), so that a boundary whose best candidate '
        'is paired with another boundary is left unpaired rather than paired with a lower-scoring candidate. Because a '
        '50% identity threshold may be permissive, particularly for short exons, all analyses were repeated with '
        'orthologous exons defined by at least 70% or 80% identity, and by 50% identity with reciprocal best matches '
        'only (Results 3.1 and 3.3, Table S1).',
    26: 'The ratio-based score is calculated as the average percent identity of the orthologous exons, multiplied by '
        'the ratio of the lower to the higher percent identity between the human and mouse exons, thereby favoring '
        'exons pairs with both high overall similarity and balanced conservation across species. A human splicing '
        'junction between the end boundary of human exon A and the start boundary of downstream human exon B is '
        'defined as orthologous to a mouse splicing junction between the end boundary of mouse exon A\' and the start '
        'boundary of downstream mouse exon B\' if exons A and A\' are orthologous, and exons B and B\' are orthologous, '
        'and exon A precedes exon B and exon A\' precedes exon B\' (Figure 1A).',

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ methods 2.2 (datasets)
    29: 'To test for differential splicing between human and mouse, a search was done in GEO '
        '(https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/) (36) to find RNA-sequencing datasets that profiled at least four naive '
        'immune cell types: B cells, T cells, natural killer (NK) cells and monocytes (Mono), and include at least '
        'seven samples. Specifically, the search query: "b-lymphocytes" [MeSH Terms] AND "t-lymphocytes" [MeSH '
        'Terms] AND "killer cells, natural" [MeSH Terms] AND "monocytes" [MeSH Terms] AND ("7"[n_samples]: '
        '"100000"[n_samples]) AND "Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing" [Filter] was used. That '
        'query on 12/5/2026 returned 17 mouse datasets and 56 human datasets. After filtering out single cell '
        'datasets, SuperSerieses, datasets without naïve immune cells, datasets with low depth, datasets with too short '
        'reads, and datasets with less than four cell types, one mouse dataset and six human datasets remained. One mouse and one human '
        'dataset were profiled by the same lab, GSE115736 and GSE116177 (37), termed H2 and M1. This pair was used as'
        ' an example of a human-mouse comparison of PSI (Figure 3D). To separate species '
        'differences from dataset differences, two additional human datasets were used: GSE60424 (38), termed H1 '
        '(healthy control samples only), and GSE118165 ({calderon}), termed H3 (unstimulated samples only). A second '
        'mouse dataset, termed M2, was assembled per cell type from datasets of sorted cells from unmanipulated or control mice of '
        'different genomic backgrounds: follicular B cells from GSE287527, NK cells and Ly6C+ monocytes of '
        'phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) treated control mice from GSE261448, naive CD4 T cells from ENCODE '
        '(ENCSR968ZOA and ENCSR835KFT) ({encode}), and CD8 T cells of uninfected wild-type mice from GSE313371. '
        'Human-Mouse fibroblasts RNA-seq was taken from E-MTAB-5919 dataset (40) that includes dermal fibroblasts '
        'from both human and mouse. All datasets have at least two replicates in all cell types. The samples used, '
        'their strain, tissue source, sorting markers, cell state, treatment and replicate identity are detailed in '
        'Table S2. The datasets considered in the search for additional datasets, and the reason each candidate was '
        'not used, are listed in Table S3. Raw sequencing reads were downloaded from the NCBI Sequence Read Archive '
        '(SRA) (41) using the NCBI SRA Toolkit (v3.0.0, Available from: https://github.com/ncbi/sra-tools), or from '
        'the ENCODE portal. Reads of H3 and M2 were trimmed with the same cutadapt (v2.6) ({cutadapt}) command for all '
        'samples, which removes Nextera and TruSeq adapter sequences from the 3\' end and the Smart-seq2 ISPCR '
        'oligonucleotide from the 5\' end of each read (all three in every sample), low quality ends (quality < 20) '
        'and reads shorter than 30 nucleotides. Hisat2 (42) (version 2.0.5) was used to align human or mouse RNA-seq '
        'reads to the Homo sapiens genome (GRCh38) or Mus musculus genome (GRCm39), respectively. The resulting '
        'binary alignment map (BAM) files were sorted and indexed by SAMtools (43). To evaluate the quality of the '
        'data, the flagstat option of SAMtools was used. In H1, H2, M1 and the fibroblasts, all samples had at least '
        '17 million properly mapped reads and more than 90% of the reads were mapped; in H3 and M2, 76-97% of the '
        'reads were aligned (Tables S2, S4). All samples were used for the analysis.',

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ methods 2.3 (junctions, events)
    34: 'To ensure robust detection and cross-dataset comparability, only orthologous junctions that fulfil the '
        'following criteria were included in the analysis: (1) Junction must be covered by at least one read in at '
        'least two human and two mouse immune cell datasets, and (2) junction must be covered by at least 10 reads in '
        'at least two samples in at least two datasets. These criteria minimize spurious junctions and increase the '
        'likelihood of sufficient evidence across datasets. The remaining junctions were assigned to LeafCutter '
        'clusters. A LeafCutter cluster is a set of junctions that share start or end coordinates, and it represents '
        'an alternative splicing event. Junctions that were not assigned to clusters, that is, junctions that do not '
        'share start or end genomic locations with other junctions, were removed. The fibroblasts dataset was not '
        'part of the junction definition step, and the junction spanning reads counts of fibroblasts were considered '
        'only for those same junctions. Events were classified as skipped exon (two inclusion junctions and a skipping '
        'junction), alternative 3\' splice site (two or more junctions that share their start, the 5\' splice site, in '
        'the direction of transcription, and differ in their 3\' splice site), alternative 5\' splice site (two or '
        'more junctions that share their end, the 3\' splice site, and differ in their 5\' splice site), in both cases '
        'with the two alternative splice sites on the same exon (an annotated exon of the human Ensembl 116 annotation '
        'contains both), alternative first or last exon (two or more junctions that share one splice site and whose '
        'alternative splice sites are on different exons, one of which is the first or the last exon of an annotated '
        'transcript), or complex. The event type is used to describe the events (Results 3.1) and for a sensitivity '
        'analysis: for alternative 3\' and 5\' splice site events, the correspondence between the human and mouse '
        'splice sites was considered well supported if the distance between the two alternative splice sites is '
        'identical in the two species (for example, two acceptors three nucleotides apart in both human and mouse); '
        'for skipped exons, if the skipped exon has the same length in both species. Complex events and '
        'alternative first and last exons were not assessed for splice-site correspondence.',

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ methods 2.4 (LeafCutter, single pairs)
    37: 'Using the JSR expression table, differential splicing analysis was performed according to the workflow of '
        'LeafCutter (44) (https://davidaknowles.github.io/leafcutter/articles/Usage.html), separately for each '
        'cell type and each pair of datasets: the six human-mouse pairs (H1, H2 or H3 vs M1 or M2), the three '
        'human-human pairs (H1-H2, H2-H3, H1-H3) and the mouse-mouse pair (M1-M2, Figure S1A), and for human vs mouse '
        'fibroblasts. The human exon annotation of Ensembl release 116 was used to label the clusters with gene names. '
        'Default parameters were used, except for -g and -i. The -g parameter (min_samples_per_group) sets the minimal '
        'number of samples in each group that are covered by at least min_coverage reads. The -i parameter '
        '(min_samples_per_intron) sets the minimum number of samples in which an intron must have at least one '
        'supporting read to be retained; introns that do not meet this threshold are excluded. Both parameters are '
        'assigned a number which is 60% of the number of samples in the smallest group in the relevant comparison, and '
        'at least two (2-3, Tables S4, S5).',
    38: 'The fraction of JSRs of a junction in each LeafCutter cluster in each sample is termed the percent spliced '
        'in (PSI). Differential splicing is measured in terms of the change in PSI between conditions, ΔPSI. '
        'Max(|ΔPSI|) was defined as the maximal abs(ΔPSI) value in each comparison, in each LeafCutter cluster. In the '
        'comparisons of two datasets (or two groups of samples), a similar use ratio was assessed by an equivalence '
        'test, which requires positive evidence of similarity rather than the absence of a significant difference: '
        'the PSI of each junction was calculated in each sample with at least 20 JSRs in the cluster (the LeafCutter '
        'min_coverage), and the difference between the two group means was estimated with its 90% confidence interval '
        '(CI) by Welch\'s t-statistic ({welch}). An event is equivalent if, for every junction, the whole 90% CI lies '
        'within ±0.1 (the equivalence margin, the same as the ΔPSI threshold for differential splicing), which is '
        'identical to rejecting, at α = 0.05, both the hypothesis that the ΔPSI is at least 0.1 and the hypothesis '
        'that it is at most -0.1 (two one-sided tests) ({schuirmann}, {lakens}). An event that is not significantly '
        'different but whose CI exceeds ±0.1 is therefore not equivalent but inconclusive.',
    41: 'Based on the differential splicing analysis, in each comparison of two datasets of the same species, in the '
        'fibroblast comparison and in the sex, age and tissue comparisons (Supplementary Note 1), each alternative '
        'splicing event was assigned a status (Figure S1C): Not testable: fewer than the minimal number of samples '
        '(the -g value) in a group with at least 20 JSRs; Differentially spliced: LeafCutter p.adjust ≤ 0.05 and '
        'max(|ΔPSI|) ≥ 0.1; Equivalent: for every junction of the event, the 90% CI of the ΔPSI lies entirely within '
        '±0.1; Inconclusive: a testable event that is neither differentially spliced nor equivalent. The p.adjust of '
        'the event is the Benjamini-Hochberg adjusted P value reported by LeafCutter. The ΔPSI threshold of 0.1 is '
        'commonly used for splicing differences, and the equivalence margin was set to the same value, so that an event '
        'cannot be both differentially spliced and equivalent. The human-mouse dataset pairs were analyzed by LeafCutter as well, but only to estimate '
        'effect sizes (ΔPSI and PSI correlations), not to classify events.',
    42: 'Human-mouse classification of the events in immune cells. To classify each event in each immune cell type '
        'between species, using all three human and both mouse datasets together, each event was represented by a '
        'single junction: the junction with the largest range of dataset PSIs (maximum minus minimum over the five '
        'datasets, without using the species of the datasets), where the PSI of a dataset is the mean PSI over its '
        'samples with at least 20 JSRs in the cluster. As the PSI is calculated from all the reads of '
        'the event, a sample has either a PSI for all junctions of an event or for none, and an event was considered '
        'testable in a cell type if it had a PSI in at least two samples of each of the five datasets. The testable '
        'events were then classified in three steps (Figure 1B). First, unchanged events: a one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) '
        'of the PSI of the junction over the samples, with the five datasets as groups (scipy.stats.f_oneway), was not '
        'significant (Benjamini-Hochberg adjusted P > 0.05 over the testable events of the cell type), and in every '
        'junction of the event the dataset PSIs lay within a range (maximum minus minimum) of at most 0.1. Unchanged '
        'events were not tested further. Second, the PSI of the junction in all samples of the three human datasets was '
        'compared with its PSI in all samples of the two mouse datasets by a two-sided Wilcoxon rank-sum '
        '(Mann-Whitney) test, with Benjamini-Hochberg correction over the events tested in this step. Third, the absolute '
        'difference in dataset PSI of the junction was calculated for each of the ten dataset pairs, and averaged over '
        'the six human-mouse pairs and over the four within-species pairs (three human-human and one mouse-mouse). An '
        'event was classified as differentially spliced between species if the Wilcoxon adjusted P ≤ 0.05, the mean '
        'human-mouse |ΔPSI| ≥ 0.1, and the mean human-mouse |ΔPSI| was more than twice the mean within-species |ΔPSI|. '
        'The last criterion is needed because the samples of a dataset are not independent replicates, so that the '
        'Wilcoxon test alone also detects differences between laboratories and protocols; requiring the human-mouse '
        'difference to be at least twice the difference between datasets of the same species ensures that it is not '
        'explained by dataset differences. All other testable events were considered inconclusive. The usage of the '
        'minor isoform of an event was defined as the PSI of the less used isoform (for skipped exons, the smaller of '
        'the inclusion and exclusion fractions), averaged over the five datasets (Table S6). As there is a single '
        'fibroblast '
        'dataset in each species, this classification cannot be applied to fibroblasts; they were classified by the '
        'status of the single human-mouse fibroblast comparison, as described above.',
    45: 'Functional enrichment analyses were performed using gseapy enrich module (45) with three MSigDB (46) gene set '
        'collections: GO_Biological_Process_2025 (c5.go.bp), Reactome_2022 (c2.cp.reactome) and Hallmark (h.all; '
        'MSigDB) version v2024.1.Hs, with a background of the 1,932 genes that contain at least one event testable in '
        'at least one immune cell type. Two cell-type-independent gene lists were defined by pooling the '
        'classifications of all events of a gene across the five immune cell types: genes with at least one '
        'differentially spliced classification and no unchanged classification (n = 698), and genes with at least one '
        'unchanged classification and no differentially spliced classification (n = 325). Enrichment was tested for '
        'each of the lists against each of the three gene-set libraries. Benjamini–Hochberg False Discovery Rate (FDR) '
        'correction (47) was applied per annotation source. Enrichment results were considered statistically '
        'significant at FDR ≤ 0.1. As highly expressed genes are more often testable and classified (Table S7), we '
        'tested whether an enrichment is explained by coverage: list membership was modeled by logistic regression on '
        'term membership and the gene\'s event coverage (log10 mean JSRs per sample), and the term was considered '
        'independent of coverage if its coefficient remained significant. Similarly, to answer the reviewers\' concern '
        'that expression level biases the results, the effect of event coverage, number of junctions and number of '
        'events per gene on testability and on the classification was tested by logistic regression with cell type as '
        'a covariate, using statsmodels ({statsmodels}) (Table S7).',

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ results 3.1
    49: 'There are 16,092 high confidence human-mouse one-to-one orthologous genes in Ensembl 116, out of which '
        '15,203 have at least two exons in each species, and 14,769 have at least two orthologous exons. Considering '
        'all exons from all the transcripts of each orthologous gene, those genes in human include 804,930 unique '
        'exons, out of which 544,278 exons (68%) have mouse orthologs. In mouse, those genes contain 666,955 unique '
        'exons, out of which 494,647 exons (74%) have human orthologs. The lower fraction of human exons with mouse '
        'orthologs is due to the larger number of annotated exons in human (48, 49). There are 390,686 unique '
        'orthologous exons boundaries with orthologous exons defined by at least 50% identity. If the identity '
        'threshold is increased to at least 70% or 80% identity, 354,994 and 302,556 boundaries remain, respectively. '
        'If a reciprocal best match is required, 392,108 boundaries are paired (390,630 of them also with 50% '
        'identity); the number is slightly higher because removing the non-reciprocal exon pairs leaves some '
        'boundaries without a competing candidate in the mutual-best pairing of boundaries (Methods 2.1) (Table S1). '
        'Although exon rank was not used to define exon orthology, the orthologous exon boundaries used by the '
        'alternative splicing events were consistently ordered: of the 13,517 boundaries in exons of the canonical '
        'transcripts of both species, 85% had the same exon rank in human and mouse, and 97% had a constant rank '
        'offset within the gene, for example mouse exon n+1 for human exon n (Table S1, sheet rank_consistency).',
    50: 'We have used three human and two mouse RNA-seq datasets for each immune cell type: B cells, CD4 T cells, CD8 '
        'T cells, natural killer cells, and monocytes (37, 38, {calderon}, {encode}), later termed B, T4, T8, NK and '
        'Mo, respectively, to compare alternative splicing use ratio between human and mouse in the immune system '
        '(Figure 1B, Tables S4, S2).',
    52: 'The alternative splicing events were tested in each immune cell type for differential splicing between the '
        'three human datasets, GSE60424 (H1), GSE115736 (H2) and GSE118165 (H3) (three human-human pairs), and between '
        'the two mouse datasets, GSE116177 (M1) and M2, which is composed of four datasets (one mouse-mouse pair for '
        'each cell type) (Figure S1A), and classified in each comparison as not testable, inconclusive, equivalent or '
        'differentially spliced (Figure S1C, Figures 2A, B, Table S9).',
    54: '3.2\tFor about a fifth of the testable alternative splicing events, the use ratio differs between human '
        'and mouse more than between datasets of the same species',
    55: 'In the Human-Human comparisons of all immune cell types, a median of 27-34% of the testable alternative '
        'splicing events were differentially spliced, and in the Mouse-Mouse comparison, 14-27% (Table 1, Figures '
        '2A, B). Human-mouse differences were larger than the differences between datasets of the same species '
        '(48-52% differentially spliced, median over the six human-mouse pairs; Figure 2C). This result is '
        'independent of significance thresholds: restricting the analysis to the 1,752-1,982 events per cell type '
        'that were testable in all ten comparisons, the max(|ΔPSI|) of an event was larger between species than '
        'within species (paired Wilcoxon test, P < 10^-94 in every cell type; Figure 3A, in which each dataset pair '
        'is shown separately), and junction PSI values were less correlated between human and mouse (Pearson r = '
        '0.76-0.89) than between datasets of the same species (r = 0.88-0.97; Figures 3B, D, Table S6).',
    56: 'The relatively high fraction of within species differential splicing in human (22-39%) and mouse (14-27%) '
        'immune cells (Table 1) suggests that the detection of differential splicing is affected by multiple '
        'experimental variables, for example by the cell extraction and sorting protocols, and the sequencing '
        'technology. Moreover, failure to detect a significant difference does not demonstrate that the use ratio is '
        'the same: within species, an equivalent use ratio (within ±0.1) could be demonstrated for a median of only '
        '12-38% of the testable events, and most of the remaining events were inconclusive (Figures 2A, B, Table S9). '
        'As a comparison of a single human and a single mouse dataset (Figure 2C) cannot separate species differences '
        'from dataset differences, we classified each event in each cell type based on the PSI values of one junction '
        'in all five datasets (Methods 2.5, Figure 1B): 2,822 events in 1,932 genes had a PSI in at least two samples '
        'of each of the three human and two mouse datasets, and were therefore testable between species, in at least '
        'one cell type. An event was classified as unchanged if the ANOVA over the five datasets was not significant '
        'and its PSI varied (maximum minus minimum) by at most 0.1 across all five datasets, and as differentially '
        'spliced if all human samples differed significantly from all mouse samples (Wilcoxon test) by a mean '
        'difference of at least 0.1 that was more than twice the mean difference between datasets of the same '
        'species. Out of the 1,799-2,043 events that were testable in each cell type, 342-431 (18.8-21.1%) were '
        'differentially spliced and 182-241 (10.1-12.5%) were unchanged (Figures 2D, S2, Table 1, Tables S10, S11). For '
        'the differentially spliced events, the mean difference between datasets of the same species was much smaller '
        'than the mean human-mouse difference (median 0.06-0.08 vs 0.23-0.29), whereas the unchanged events differed '
        'little, as required by their definition, in all comparisons (median mean human-mouse |ΔPSI| 0.02; Figure S3). '
        'Nevertheless, counting each event separately in each immune cell type in which it was classified, 56% of the '
        'differentially spliced human-mouse classifications were differentially spliced, by a smaller ΔPSI, also in '
        'at least one within-species pair. An example is exon 5 skipping in PUF60, which is differentially spliced '
        'between human and mouse in CD8 T cells, B cells, NK cells and monocytes, inconclusive in CD4 T cells, in which '
        'H3 resembles the mouse datasets, and differs also between some datasets of the same species (Figure S4).',
    58: '3.3\tHuman-mouse differential splicing events are often shared between immune cell types',
    59: 'Of the 1,013 events that are differentially spliced in at least one immune cell type, 486 (48%) are '
        'differentially spliced in more than one immune cell type, and 45 in all five (Figure 2E, Table S11), '
        'including the alternative 3\' splice site in RPL22L1 (three cell types; Figure S5), the skipped exons in UCKL1 '
        '(four cell types; Figure S6) and ZNF207 (five cell types), and an alternative 5\' splice site in SRSF7 (five '
        'cell types). In the other cell types, the 527 events that are differentially spliced in only one cell type '
        'were not testable in all datasets (49%), inconclusive (48%) or unchanged (3%) (Table S6). Thus, differential '
        'splicing in a single cell type mostly reflects coverage rather than cell type specificity. An example is an '
        'alternative 3\' splice site in USP16 (Figure 4), which is differentially spliced in CD4 T cells, CD8 T cells '
        'and B cells, and not testable in NK cells and monocytes. The genes containing only differentially spliced '
        'events (pooled over the five immune cell types) are not enriched for any functional term. The classification '
        'was robust to the definition of exon orthology: with at least 70% and 80% exon identity and with reciprocal '
        'best matches, 933, 764 and 1,019 events were differentially spliced in at least one cell type (compared with '
        '1,013 at 50% identity), and in each cell type 19.0-21.1%, 18.6-20.3% and 19.0-21.1% of the testable events, '
        'compared with 18.8-21.1% (Table S6); with events with well-supported splice-site correspondence, 17.6-19.8% '
        'of the testable events were differentially spliced, compared with 18.8-21.1% of all events (Table S6). '
        'Testability and classification depended on coverage: events with higher coverage were more likely to be '
        'testable and to be classified as unchanged, and less likely to be classified as differentially spliced, and '
        'events with more junctions were less likely to be classified as unchanged (Table S7).',
    61: '3.4\tAn unchanged use ratio is found mostly in events dominated by one isoform',
    62: 'There are 552 alternative splicing events with an unchanged use ratio in human and mouse in at least one '
        'cell type (Table S11). Out of those, 72, 63, 53, 48 and 43 events are unchanged only in monocytes, CD8 T '
        'cells, B cells, NK cells and CD4 T cells, respectively. There are 36 alternative splicing events that are '
        'unchanged across the five immune cell types, 8 of them in 8 ribosomal protein genes, and others in genes of '
        'the translation machinery (EEF1A1, RACK1) and of RNA splicing (RBM39, SRSF7, PRPF40A, SNRPG) (Figures 2F, G). '
        'Most unchanged events are dominated by one isoform – the junction with the lower number of reads accounted '
        'for less than 5% of the reads in 87% of the 1,113 unchanged classifications (each event counted in each cell '
        'type in which it was unchanged), which are 12% of all 9,528 testable classifications. Conversely, of the '
        '4,967 testable classifications in which the minor isoform accounted for at least 5%, only 2.9% were '
        'unchanged, and 29.3% were differentially spliced, compared with 7.3% of the classifications in which the '
        'minor isoform accounted for less than 5% (Figure 3C, Table S6). Thus, while species differences are found '
        'mostly among events in which both isoforms are used, an unchanged use ratio was found mostly where one isoform '
        'dominates. Exon 7 skipping in SRSF7 (Figure 5) is an example of an event with a low but similar use of the '
        'minor isoform in both species: the skipping isoform accounts for about 9-15% of the JSRs of the event in most '
        'datasets, and the event is unchanged in B cells and NK cells and inconclusive in the other immune cell '
        'types.',
    63: 'The list of genes containing unchanged events and no differentially spliced events (n = 325) was '
        'significantly enriched for 24 Reactome terms and one hallmark gene set (FDR ≤ 0.1), related to translation '
        '(eukaryotic translation elongation, SRP-dependent cotranslational protein targeting), cellular responses to '
        'stimuli and MYC targets (Table S12), whereas the longer list of genes containing only differentially spliced '
        'events (n = 698) was not enriched for any term. As ribosomal protein genes are highly expressed and coverage '
        'affects classification (Table S7), we tested whether the enrichment is explained by coverage (Methods 2.6): '
        'several terms remained associated with unchanged events after adjusting for coverage, for example cellular '
        'response to chemical stress (adjusted odds ratio 2.85, P = 0.0006), signaling by WNT (2.38, P = 0.003) and MYC '
        'targets (1.92, P = 0.014; Table S12). The lists were defined per gene, pooling all events and cell types: '
        '160 genes contain both a differentially spliced and an unchanged event and are in neither list.',
    66: 'Human-mouse differential splicing events can be due to a difference in splicing code between human and '
        'mouse, in which case such events will be found in all cell types that express the gene, or due to a change '
        'in splicing regulation, in which case they may be found only in some cell types. As we found many '
        'human-mouse differential splicing events that are shared between immune cell types, we wanted to extend '
        'beyond the immune system. Thus, we compared the human-mouse splicing ratio between immune cells and dermal '
        'fibroblasts of human and mouse that were cultured and sequenced in the same study (Methods 2.2) (40). Out of '
        'the 4,570 orthologous alternative splicing events, 3,801 (83%) were testable between human and mouse '
        'fibroblasts; 1,048 (28%) of them were differentially spliced, 1,748 (46%) were equivalent and 1,005 (26%) '
        'were inconclusive (Table 1). Out of the 297-385 events that were differentially spliced in each immune cell '
        'type and testable in fibroblasts, 38-47% are differentially spliced also in fibroblasts, 31-39% are '
        'equivalent, and 22-24% are inconclusive (Figure 6A). Out of the 164-222 events that were unchanged in each '
        'immune cell type and testable in fibroblasts, 84-92% are equivalent in fibroblasts (Figure 6B). Conversely, '
        'out of the 298-333 events differentially spliced in fibroblasts that are testable in each immune cell type, '
        '39-49% are differentially spliced in the immune cell type and only 1-3% unchanged (Figure 6C), and out of the '
        '944-1,058 events with an equivalent use ratio in fibroblasts, 10-14% are differentially spliced and 15-20% '
        'unchanged in immune cell types (Figure 6D). Events that were differentially spliced in more immune cell types '
        'were more often differentially spliced also in fibroblasts (59% of the 113 events differentially spliced in '
        'four or five immune cell types and testable in fibroblasts, compared with 26% of the events not differentially '
        'spliced in any immune cell type; Table S11), and 35 of the 36 events unchanged in all five immune cell types were equivalent in '
        'fibroblasts.',

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ discussion
    69: 'Here, we performed a systematic comparison of alternative splicing usage between human and mouse across five '
        'immune cell types, each profiled in three human and two mouse RNA-seq datasets. We identified 4,570 '
        'orthologous alternative splicing events that are expressed in immune cells; in each cell type, 18.8-21.1% of '
        'the testable events were differentially spliced between species, and only 10.1-12.5% displayed an unchanged '
        'use ratio, mostly events in which one isoform accounts for more than 95% of the reads. Thus, even when a gene '
        'and its exons are orthologous and the junctions of an alternative splicing event are expressed in both '
        'species, that is, when the gene sequence, its expression and its alternative splicing repertoire are '
        'conserved, the use ratio of the event often differs between human and mouse, with implications for how mouse '
        'models are interpreted in immunological research. Human-mouse differences were less frequent in fibroblasts, '
        'but the most robust differences in immune cells were often present also in fibroblasts.',
    70: 'The splicing differences between human and mouse datasets were larger, and the PSI values less correlated, '
        'than between datasets of the same species. While this indicates that the human-mouse difference in splicing '
        'ratio is not merely due to technical differences between RNA-seq datasets, the within-species comparisons '
        'also showed that many events change due to dataset differences (22-39% and 14-27% differentially spliced '
        'within human and mouse). Thus, we based the interpretation of species divergence on events whose human and '
        'mouse samples differed significantly, by a mean difference at least twice the mean difference between '
        'datasets of the same species: 342-431 per cell type (1,013 events in total). A test of all human against all '
        'mouse samples alone would treat the samples of a dataset as independent replicates and would therefore also '
        'detect differences between laboratories and protocols; the requirement that the human-mouse difference '
        'exceeds the within-species differences by at least twofold is what separates species from dataset '
        'differences. Many of the differentially spliced events (56% of the classifications) were differentially '
        'spliced, by a smaller ΔPSI, also between datasets of the same species. Thus, the use ratio of many of the '
        'events that differ between species also varies between datasets of the same species, and for most events, '
        'the dataset differences are too large to determine whether the use ratio is conserved. Consequently, 67-69% '
        'of the testable events in each cell type were inconclusive, so that the fraction of differentially spliced '
        'events (19-21%) reflects the stringency of the classification rather than the fraction of events whose use '
        'ratio differs between species; among the events that could be classified, differentially spliced events '
        'outnumbered unchanged events in every cell type (1.5-2.0-fold; Table 1). The consistency of the results with '
        'stricter definitions of exon orthology and with events with well-supported splice-site correspondence (Table '
        'S6) argues that the species difference is not an artifact of the orthology assignment.',
    71: 'Of the 1,013 events that are differentially spliced between human and mouse in at least one immune cell '
        'type, 45 are shared between all five cell types: 27 skipped exons, ten complex events, three alternative 3\' '
        'and three alternative 5\' splice sites and two alternative first exons, for example in the splicing factors '
        'SRSF7, SRRM1 and MBNL1, the RNA-binding proteins ILF2, RALY and SAFB, and the ribosomal protein RPS24. The alternative 3\' splice sites of RPL22L1 (Figure S5) and USP16 (Figure 4), which are '
        'differentially spliced in three immune cell types each, are three nucleotides apart. In USP16, a histone H2A '
        'deubiquitinase, the three additional nucleotides code for an additional Alanine at the beginning of exon 6, '
        'upstream of Cysteine 205 that is required for USP16 deubiquitinase activity (50). In the ribosome-associated '
        'protein RPL22L1 (Figure S5), the three additional nucleotides add Glutamine. Whether these differences affect '
        'protein function remains to be tested. Genes containing NAGNAG events, that is, AG dinucleotides that are '
        'separated by one nucleotide, are conserved between human and mouse and were found to be enriched for DNA '
        'binding genes (51). In USP16, the shorter exon was more frequent in human immune cells. Testing all testable '
        'alternative 3\' and 5\' splice site events, events with alternative splice sites up to six nucleotides apart '
        'were not more often differentially spliced between species than events with more distant splice sites '
        '(alternative 3\' splice sites: 16.3% vs 17.9%, Fisher\'s exact test, P = 0.50; alternative 5\' splice sites: '
        '29.3% vs 22.9%, P = 0.23; Table S6).',
    72: 'An unchanged use ratio between human and mouse could be demonstrated for 552 events in 485 genes, most of '
        'which are events in which one isoform accounts for more than 95% of the reads. The genes carrying the '
        'alternative splicing events with an unchanged ratio were enriched for translation related mechanisms, mostly '
        'due to ribosomal proteins. An example of an alternative splicing event with a low but similar use of the minor '
        'isoform in both species is exon 7 skipping in the serine/arginine-rich splicing factor SRSF7 (Figure 5), which '
        'removes 12 amino acids from the C-terminal arginine/serine-rich (RS) domain, and is equivalent also in '
        'fibroblasts. Genes encoding core RNA-processing and splicing machinery are generally among the most '
        'evolutionarily constrained genes in mammals, in terms of sequence ({lek}) and expression level ({brawand}). '
        'This is consistent with stronger purifying selection and intolerance to loss of function compared to genes '
        'involved in more peripheral or context-dependent signaling pathways, such as those in immune and inflammatory '
        'response, which show greater divergence in both sequence and expression across mammals (53). It is possible '
        'that those genes are also under stronger purifying selection for splice ratio, which would predict splicing '
        'ratio conservation in all tissues and cell types. However, this hypothesis remains to be tested, as the '
        'unchanged events are mostly dominated by one isoform and are in highly expressed genes, for which a similar use '
        'ratio is easier to demonstrate, although some of the enrichment remained after adjusting for coverage.',
    73: 'To test if indeed the human-mouse conserved splicing ratio in the immune system is also conserved in other '
        'cell types, we compared immune cell types to fibroblasts, a purified non-immune cell type. Out of the events '
        'unchanged in each immune cell type, 84-92% were equivalent in fibroblasts (Figure 6B), and 35 of the 36 events '
        'unchanged in all five immune cell types are also equivalent in fibroblasts. Those events are mostly dominated '
        'by one isoform, and are in genes of core cellular processes, for example RPL11, RPS5, EEF1A1, SRSF7 and RBM39. '
        'Out of the events differentially spliced in each immune cell type, 38-47% were differentially spliced also in '
        'fibroblasts (Figure 6A). Human-mouse differences were less frequent in fibroblasts than in immune cells, '
        'although, as the fibroblast and immune datasets were generated in different studies, with different '
        'protocols and from cultured vs sorted cells, this may reflect the datasets as well as the cell type. An '
        'example of an event that differs between species in immune cells but not in fibroblasts is the skipped exon '
        'in UCKL1, which is skipped often in mouse immune cells, but is hardly skipped in human immune cells, and is '
        'similarly used in fibroblasts of both species (Figure S6). However, about half of the events that are '
        'differentially spliced between human and mouse fibroblasts (39-49%) are also differentially spliced in immune '
        'cells, and events that are differentially spliced in more immune cell types were more often differentially '
        'spliced also in fibroblasts (59% of the events differentially spliced in four or five immune cell types, '
        'compared with 26% of the events not differentially spliced in any immune cell type), for example USP16 '
        '(Figure 4), which is differentially spliced in three immune cell types, and PUF60 (Figure S4), which is '
        'differentially spliced in four immune cell types, are differentially spliced also in fibroblasts. Thus, '
        'robust human-mouse differences are often shared between cell types, and may reflect changes in cis-regulatory '
        'sequence or splicing code, while other differences may reflect immune-specific regulation. As only one human '
        'and one mouse fibroblast dataset were analyzed, the fibroblasts were cultured whereas the immune cells were '
        'sorted ex vivo, and many events were not testable in fibroblasts, these comparisons are supportive rather than '
        'definitive evidence for cell type specificity.',
    74: None,   # merged into 73 (Tal Shay)
    78: 'The ability to identify testable orthologous alternative splicing events is limited to relatively highly '
        'expressed genes, as it requires a minimal coverage of the splicing junctions by RNA-seq reads. Thus, only '
        '2,822 alternative splicing events, within 1,932 genes, were testable in at least one cell type, and our '
        'conclusions refer to such sufficiently expressed and testable orthologous alternative splicing events in the '
        'analyzed datasets rather than to the entire human-mouse alternative splicing landscape. Of these genes, 1,311 '
        'have a single testable event, whereas 621 carry 2-7 testable orthologous alternative splicing events, which '
        'often display different statuses. SRSF7 discussed above carries four orthologous alternative splicing events '
        '– a skipped exon, two alternative 5\' splice sites and one alternative 3\' splice site; the skipped exon '
        '(Figure 5) and one of the alternative 5\' splice sites are unchanged (the latter in all five immune cell '
        'types), whereas the other alternative 5\' splice site is differentially spliced in all five immune cell types '
        'and in fibroblasts, and the alternative 3\' splice site is inconclusive. Much deeper sequencing is required to '
        'compare splicing ratio of most orthologous alternative splicing events.',
    80: 'Several technical and biological factors may have influenced both the number and identity of the detected '
        'differentially spliced and unchanged alternative splicing events between human and mouse orthologous genes. '
        'First, sequencing depth directly affects the number of testable alternative splicing events between and '
        'within species, and coverage affected classification (Table S7). Second, the number of biological replicates '
        'affect statistical power, and in some datasets and cell types there were only two to four replicates. Third, '
        'with three human and two mouse datasets, the species difference cannot be tested at the level of datasets '
        'with useful power; the Wilcoxon test uses the samples of the datasets as replicates, and the requirement of a '
        'twofold larger difference between species than between datasets of the same species is a threshold rather '
        'than a statistical test of the dataset effect. Fourth, sequencing-related biases may differ between datasets '
        'due to differences in library preparation protocols (polyA, total RNA and Smart-seq2), read quality, read '
        'length, and alignment efficiency. Fifth, biological heterogeneity also represents an important source of '
        'variation. Human samples inherently contain substantial genetic variation between donors, which may increase '
        'inter-individual variability in splicing patterns compared to inbred mouse strains. Furthermore, some '
        'annotated cell populations, such as total CD4 and CD8 T cells in H1, H2 and part of M1 and M2, contain '
        'distinct cellular subpopulations with unique transcriptional and splicing programs, potentially masking or '
        'diluting species-shared splicing signatures. Additional factors such as differences in sampled tissues '
        '(peripheral blood in human; spleen, lymph nodes, bone marrow and peritoneal cavity in mouse), developmental '
        'stage/age (adults in human, 5-12 weeks or a few months in mouse), sex composition and mouse genetic '
        'background (C57BL/6, B6129 or B6CASTF1 hybrid) (Table S2) may further contribute to species-specific splicing '
        'patterns and complicate the interpretation of similar versus different use ratio of alternative splicing '
        'events. However, comparisons within each species that differed only in sex, age or tissue of origin showed '
        'far smaller splicing differences than the human-mouse comparisons, and even smaller than those between '
        'datasets of the same species (Supplementary Note 1, Figure S7), indicating that these factors do not explain '
        'the human-mouse differences. Sixth, RNA-sequencing displays sequence dependent coverage bias (62). This bias '
        'is canceled out for within species differential splicing, but between species comparison of splicing '
        'junctions spanned by different sequences with different bias may inflate the estimate of differentially '
        'spliced events.',
    81: 'To partially account for some of those sources of variability and improve the robustness of our analysis, '
        'we used several independent datasets per species, and required events that are differentially spliced '
        'between species to differ significantly between all human and all mouse samples by a mean difference at '
        'least twice as large as the mean difference between datasets of the same species. Unchanged events were '
        'defined by the absence of a significant difference between any of the five datasets together with a PSI '
        'range of at most 0.1, rather than by the absence of a significant human-mouse difference alone. The remaining '
        'events, which were inconclusive, differed between species by less than twice the dataset differences or '
        'could not be tested in all datasets, may be more sensitive to technical variability, sample heterogeneity, or '
        'context-specific regulation.',
    104: 'Table 1: Classification of the orthologous alternative splicing events.',

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ figure legends
    222: 'Figure 1 - Outline of the human-mouse splicing ratio comparative analysis. A. Definition of orthology for '
         'exons, junctions and alternative splicing events for all orthologous genes was performed. Shown is an '
         'orthologous skipped exon event in human and mouse. Exons are marked by black boxes, and orthologous exons '
         'are connected by dashed lines. Splicing junctions are represented as arcs between exons, and orthologous '
         'splicing junctions are in the same color. B. The human-mouse classification of each orthologous alternative '
         'splicing event in each immune cell type, based on one junction per event (the junction with the largest '
         'range of dataset PSIs) in the three human (H1-H3) and two mouse (M1, M2) datasets: an event is testable '
         'if it has a PSI in at least two samples of each dataset; unchanged if the ANOVA over the five datasets is not '
         'significant (adjusted P > 0.05) and the dataset PSIs vary by at most 0.1; differentially spliced if all human '
         'samples differ from all mouse samples (Wilcoxon test, adjusted P ≤ 0.05) and the mean human-mouse |ΔPSI| is '
         'at least 0.1 and more than twice the mean within-species |ΔPSI|; and inconclusive otherwise. The single '
         'dataset pairs, fibroblast and sex, age and tissue comparisons are shown in Figure S1.',
    224: 'Figure 2 - Classification of human-mouse orthologous alternative splicing events based on the conservation '
         'of their use ratio. Stacked bars display the percentages of testable orthologous alternative splicing events '
         'which are differentially spliced, equivalent or inconclusive in the (A) human-human (three dataset pairs), '
         '(B) mouse-mouse (one dataset pair) and (C) human-mouse (six dataset pairs) comparisons, in which each dataset '
         'pair is tested separately (Figure S1C), and between human and mouse fibroblasts (Fb, a single dataset pair). '
         'For A and C, each percentage is the median over the dataset pairs, taken separately for each category, so '
         'that a bar may not sum exactly to 100%; the values of each pair are in Table S9. D. Human-mouse classification '
         'of the alternative splicing events from all five datasets (Methods 2.5, Figure 1B): percentage of testable '
         'events that are differentially spliced or unchanged. E. UpSet plot of the differentially spliced events '
         'shared between cell types. Only the 25 most frequent combinations of cell types are shown, covering 958 of '
         'the 1,013 events; differential splicing in a single cell type does not imply cell type specificity, as in '
         'the other cell types most of these events are not testable in all datasets or are inconclusive (Table S6). F. '
         'UpSet plot of the unchanged events. Only the 25 most frequent combinations are shown, covering 530 of the 552 '
         'events. G. Heatmap of the 434 events that are differentially spliced (green) or unchanged (blue) in at least '
         'three immune cell types, colored by their class in each immune cell type, and their status in fibroblasts '
         '(right bar). Fb – fibroblasts.',
    226: 'Figure 3 - The amplitude of human-mouse splicing differences is larger than that of within-species '
         'differences. A. Cumulative distribution of the max(|ΔPSI|) of the events testable in all ten comparisons of '
         'each cell type (n, above each panel); each line is one dataset pair. Dotted line, ΔPSI = 0.1. B. Pearson '
         'correlation of junction PSI between the two datasets of each pair, on the same events as in A; each dot is '
         'one dataset pair. C. Percentage of the testable classifications (each event in each immune cell type) that '
         'are differentially spliced or unchanged between human and mouse (Figure 1B), by the usage of the minor '
         'isoform (the PSI of the less used isoform, averaged over the five datasets). D. Density of the PSI of the '
         'testable junctions in NK cells (hexagonal bins; darker, more junctions) for one human-human (H1 vs H2), '
         'mouse-mouse (M1 vs M2) and human-mouse (H2 vs M1) comparison; line, identity; r, Pearson correlation '
         'coefficient. A, B, D: blue, human-human; orange, human-mouse; green, mouse-mouse.',
    228: 'Figure 4 - The orthologous alternative 3\' splice site event in USP16 is differentially spliced between human '
         'and mouse. A. The event at the 3\' end of intron 5 in human (top) and mouse (bottom). Exons are shown with '
         'their nucleotide sequence and the encoded amino acids of the canonical and the alternative transcripts; arcs '
         'are the two junctions, with their coordinates on the right. The canonical (dark blue) junction includes the '
         'three nucleotides marked in yellow (CAG), which code for an additional alanine (position 150 of the human '
         'canonical protein) compared with the alternative (light blue) junction. B. Junction spanning reads (JSR) of '
         'the two junctions in each sample (top) and the same counts normalized to PSI (bottom), grouped by cell type '
         'and, within each cell type, by dataset (dashed lines). Colors are as in A. M2, M1 – mouse datasets; H1, H2, '
         'H3 – human datasets; M, H – mouse and human fibroblasts; T4, T8 – CD4 and CD8 T cells; B – B cells; NK – NK '
         'cells; Mo – monocytes.',
    230: 'Figure 5 - The orthologous skipped exon event in SRSF7 gene displays a similar splicing ratio in human and '
         'mouse. A. An orthologous skipped exon event: skip of canonical exon 7, which contains 36 nt and codes for 12 '
         'amino acids in both human and mouse; introns are not to scale. B. Stacked bars display the number of junction '
         'spanning reads that cover the alternative splicing junctions in each sample (top) and the same numbers '
         'normalized to the total to display PSI values (bottom). Colors are as in A. M2, M1 – mouse datasets; H1, H2, '
         'H3 – human datasets; M, H – mouse and human fibroblasts.',
}

