# Supplementary Note 1 (Tal Shay's Supplementary_note_1.docx with her deletions accepted; PRJNA797236 added)
SUPP_NOTE_1 = [
    'To test whether differences in sex, age or tissue of origin between the human and mouse samples could explain the '
    'human-mouse differences, we compared, separately in each dataset, between samples of the same dataset (species, '
    'laboratory and protocol) and cell type that differ in a single factor (sex, age or tissue). In human: male vs '
    'female donors of sorted naive B cells, naive CD4 and CD8 T cells, NK cells and classical monocytes (GSE107011, '
    'two vs two donors, the same four donors in all five cell types) (Monaco et al., 2019), young (23-35 years) vs old '
    '(65-78 years) donors of naive CD4 T cells (GSE143213, four vs four), and CD56dim NK cells from blood vs spleen of '
    'the same three organ donors (GSE133383) (Dogra et al., 2020). In mouse: spleen B cells of female vs male '
    'B6CASTF1/J F1 hybrid mice (ENCODE ENCSR703ACF, ENCSR788PEE, ENCSR243UCH and ENCSR092BBW, eight vs eight) (ENCODE '
    'Project Consortium, 2020; as the females carry a CAST and a B6 X chromosome and the males only a B6 X chromosome, '
    'allelic differences on chromosome X may appear as sex differences; chromosome X was not excluded, so that this '
    'comparison may overestimate the sex differences), naive CD4 T cells of young (7-11 weeks) vs old (83-86 weeks) '
    'mice (GSE184496, six vs six, three of each sex), Ly49H+ NK cells from spleen vs liver of the same mice (GSE233347, '
    'three vs three), monocytes from bone marrow vs blood (GSE116177, two vs two), and Ly6Chi monocytes from blood vs '
    'spleen of male mice (PRJNA797236, four vs four). Reads were processed as for H3 and M2 (Methods 2.2), and junction '
    'counts were added for the same alternative splicing events.',
    'Each control comparison was tested as a single comparison (Figure S1C), on the events testable in the control '
    'comparison and in all six human-mouse pairs (1,376-1,886 events per comparison). Of these events, including the '
    'inconclusive and equivalent ones, only 0-0.1% were differentially spliced between male and female (human, five '
    'cell types; mouse B cells), 0-2.3% between young and old (human and mouse naive CD4 T cells), and 0-6.6% between '
    'tissues (human NK cells from blood and spleen, mouse NK cells from spleen and liver, and mouse monocytes from bone '
    'marrow and blood and from blood and spleen) (Figure S7A, Table S13). As the control comparisons had fewer '
    'replicates than some of the dataset comparisons, we also compared effect sizes, which do not depend on the number '
    'of significant calls: the median max(|ΔPSI|) was 0.016-0.044 in the controls vs 0.097-0.135 between human and '
    'mouse, and PSI correlations were 0.98-0.995 vs 0.79-0.87 (Figures S7B, C); the human-mouse differences were larger '
    'than those of every control comparison (paired Wilcoxon test, P < 0.001). Thus, sex, age and tissue of origin '
    'cannot account for the human-mouse splicing differences; they caused even smaller differences than those between '
    'datasets of the same species.',
]
